{"name":"Meteorite","hex":"#4a3b6a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"meteorite","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A metallic or stony object or body that is the remains of a meteoroid."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/meteorite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Meteorite","blocks":[{"text":"A meteorite is a rock that originated in outer space and has fallen to the surface of a planet or moon. When the original object enters the atmosphere, various factors such as friction, pressure, and chemical interactions with the atmospheric gases cause it to heat up and radiate energy. It then becomes a meteor and forms a fireball, also known as a shooting star; astronomers call the brightest examples \"bolides\". Once it settles on the larger body's surface, the meteor becomes a meteorite. Meteorites vary greatly in size. For geologists, a bolide is a meteorite large enough to create an impact crater."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Namibie_Hoba_Meteorite_05.JPG/330px-Namibie_Hoba_Meteorite_05.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meteorite","wikidata":"Q60186"},{"title":"Meteorite (Mariah Carey song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Meteorite\" is a song by the American singer Mariah Carey from her fourteenth studio album, Me. I Am Mariah... The Elusive Chanteuse (2014). It was composed by Carey and Q-Tip. A disco track, it contains a sample of the recording \"Goin' Up in Smoke\" written by Allan Felder, Norman Harris, and Ron Tyson, all of whom received songwriting credits for \"Meteorite\" as a result. It also quotes an observation by Andy Warhol that everyone will achieve 15 minutes of fame."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meteorite_(Mariah_Carey_song)","wikidata":"Q20800549"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Oriented_Taza_Meteorite.jpg/500px-Oriented_Taza_Meteorite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Oriented_Taza_Meteorite.jpg/1280px-Oriented_Taza_Meteorite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Oriented_Taza_Meteorite.jpg/500px-Oriented_Taza_Meteorite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"NWA 859 iron meteorite showing effects of atmospheric ablation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOriented_Taza_Meteorite.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Novato_Meteorite_Impact_Pit.jpg/500px-Novato_Meteorite_Impact_Pit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Novato_Meteorite_Impact_Pit.jpg/1280px-Novato_Meteorite_Impact_Pit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Novato_Meteorite_Impact_Pit.jpg/500px-Novato_Meteorite_Impact_Pit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The impact pit made by a 61.9 grams (2.18 ounces) Novato meteorite when it hit the roof of a house on 17 October 2012.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANovato_Meteorite_Impact_Pit.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Erosi%C3%B3n_meteorito_SPMN170422.jpg/500px-Erosi%C3%B3n_meteorito_SPMN170422.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Erosi%C3%B3n_meteorito_SPMN170422.jpg/500px-Erosi%C3%B3n_meteorito_SPMN170422.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Meteorite SPMN170422 shows how far atmospheric ablation can erode.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AErosi%C3%B3n_